     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum daily dose, and I don't know of any indication where that dose is safe. Going above 3,200 mg/day adds toxicity risk (GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, fluid retention, cardiovascular events) without adding analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit. I don't want to put an order into the system that I expect to harm the patient.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day), the maximum, ideally with food and with GI protection considered\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n- Ibuprofen 400 mg PO four times daily (1,600 mg/day)\n\nIf you need more pain control or anti-inflammatory effect than these give, you could add scheduled acetaminophen or another multimodal agent. A pharmacy consult could also help. If you have a protocol or trial that calls for a nonstandard dose, you can place the order directly through your institution's override or pharmacy-verification process.\n\nTell me which option you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
